# nvimdots Tutor

This is Cloud0310's personal Neovim configuration. The default [<leader>](<leader>)
key is `<Space>`{normal}. Press `<C-p>`{normal} in normal mode to open the command
and keymap picker.

# Open files and move between windows

Press `<C-n>`{normal} to toggle the file explorer. Open the **lua/config** folder,
then open **autocmds.lua** with [o](o). Press `<C-n>`{normal} again to close the
explorer.

Use `<A-i>`{normal} and `<A-o>`{normal} to switch to the next and previous buffer.
On macOS, configure your terminal's Option key to send Alt if these keys do not
reach Neovim.

Press `<C-w>v`{normal} to split the window vertically. Use `<C-h>`{normal} and
`<C-l>`{normal} to move between windows, and `<A-h>`{normal} and `<A-l>`{normal} to
resize them. Run [:close](:close) when the extra window is no longer needed.

# Find files and search text

Press `<leader>`{normal}`f`{normal}`f`{normal} to search for files in the current
working directory. Press `<leader>`{normal}`f`{normal}`p`{normal} to search text.
The search backend is selected in **lua/config/settings.lua**; text search uses
ripgrep. Press `<Enter>`{normal} to open a result.

# Format code

Open a code file and run the following command to inspect the formatter selected
for its file type:
~~~ cmd
    :ConformInfo
~~~

Press `<A-S-f>`{normal} or run `:Format` to format the buffer. A selected range can
be formatted with `:'<,'>Format`.

Press `<A-f>`{normal} to toggle format on save for the current session. To change
the default, edit **format_on_save** in **lua/config/settings.lua**. To toggle
formatting for a file type, run:
~~~ cmd
    :FormatterToggleFt lua
~~~

# Diagnostics and tests

Use `g[`{normal} and `g]`{normal} to move between diagnostics. Press
`<leader>`{normal}`l`{normal}`x`{normal} to display diagnostics for the current line.

In a project with a configured test runner, `<leader>`{normal}`T`{normal}`n`{normal}
runs the nearest test, and `<leader>`{normal}`T`{normal}`%`{normal} runs the current
file. Press `<leader>`{normal}`T`{normal}`s`{normal} to open the results tree.

# Edit the configuration

Basic keymaps and editor options live in **lua/config/**. Plugin configuration and
plugin keymaps live together in **lua/plugins/**. Edit these files directly.

Run `:Lazy` to inspect plugins, `:Mason` to inspect external tools, and `:checkhealth`
to diagnose setup problems. The repository's **docs/** directory describes tool
installation, formatting, and the configured language toolchains.

